I think the backstory of this movie is  so interesting because you and Andy knew  each other well before there was ever  gonna be a movie about your family's  story so I wonder if you can just  explain how we got to the point where  that came came to be part of the  relationship sure well I being a  producer always looking for stories it  took me a bit of time to realize that I  was sitting on rather an amazing story  the story of my own parents and I had  long wanted to create a movie but first  of all it was dependent on finding the  right writer and I saw a play called  shadow lands by Bill Nicholson and I  thought that is exactly the tone that I  think the movie should have and I  developed it for quite a long time with  him partly because when I asked him to  do it and I told him the story he said  he'd love to do it on the condition that  I didn't pay him until and unless the  film happened which is a great result  and so for six or seven years we  developed it together whenever he was  free from his other assignments and I  was free and we got it to a certain  point and at that point to my great joy  Andy Serkis and I had already formed a  company called The Imaginarium  and Andy read the script and to my huge  joy he said he asked me if he could  direct it so I thought yeah definitely  no problem now Andy you've had people  know you for such a long time for great  acting an innovative acting and so much  within that side of the business but had  juror had you always had an interest in  directing what you know how far back  does that go and it actually goes back a  very very long way before I became an  actress studied visual arts so I always  was very much in touch with trying to  tell stories visually before even before  I became an actor and over the course of  you know 25 30 years during my as my  acting career was taking off I started  to make short films and got involved of  course after meeting Peter Jackson  working on Lord of the Rings the whole  performance capture  this venture started so I started to  direct performance capture for video  games and and other projects and then  Peter asked me to direct the second unit  for the Hobbit for the Hobbit trilogy  which was about the time that Jonathan  Ive formed The Imaginarium and then  finally as we were laying out our stall  of things that we want to make and form  this performance capture studio and  production entity at the same time you  know it was very much with an idea that  Jonathan would produce films that I  would direct and and this this falls  into that category although and it's  become my directorial debut this film  but in actual fact we'd already started  working on another production the jungle  book which which we'd shot and was in  post-production then there was this  incredible window of opportunity when  the most incredible two actors were  available and that one of them is  sitting on my left Andrew how did this  first cross your radar and was there a  specific aspect of it that you just  tapped into you said I have to do this  it's so magical I read this script and  it was unlike anything I've ever read  they bill Nicholson and somehow managed  to take this life this larger-than-life  existence and somehow contain it in a  120 page script and it didn't and it  felt like I don't know if L allegorical  it felt like it was it was it was bigger  if it just felt bigger than a whole life  somehow they managed in the how Bill  wrote it he managed to find all of the  God the cosmic mysterious connection  that these two people had and their  connection to the world it went beyond  to human beings and I just sobbed I just  kind of read read this thing and sobbed  and and and laughed and sobbed and  laughed and you know interchangeably and  sometimes both at the same time and I I  kind of saw a template of how to  if i thought well god i want my life to  feel like this so maybe maybe by  apprenticing to robin and i can rub off  on me and then i and I spoke to Andy and  that was just a wonderful conversation  he put he put any fears or kind of  concerns I had at rest and they had  dinner with Jonathan and in this city we  went to a wonderful restaurant called  animal I don't know if you've been but  I'd recommend it if you're not  vegetarian and I I met I met an  embodiment of these two people I met I  met what these two magical people  created and they created an another  magical person and I thought well a few  months with this person would be a  lovely time and what an honour if he  ultimately wants me to play his father  what an honor I want to ask Jonathan and  Andy if you can tell us about the other  key element of the film who's not here  Claire Foy I think it's amazing that you  know now it's I think now it's like it's  obvious in the cool choice if you can  get Claire Foy to do anything after the  crown you know you do it but this was  before anyone had seen any of that so  how did you guys realize that she was  right for for this part pick her out of  a lot of other people I'm sure would  have wanted to do it well I think the  first time I sort of saw her I've had I  mean Andy's actually worked with her  before and knew her well personally and  knew her work probably better than me I  went to some awards ceremony and the  very first trailer for the crown was  shown because somebody was getting some  award had worked on it and I looked at  Claire playing the Queen and my mother  is a not unrealistic extraordinary  so we then very quickly met her and it  was very clear to both of us that you  know not only is she extraordinary  extraordinary person and an amazing  actress but that she had some of the  things in common with my mother  beauty bravery living in the moment  which is I think a critical shared thing  not looking back not looking for just  being there bang bang plunk in the  moment which is  something we'd all like to learn to be I  think Andrea I want to ask you I know  from just various things I've read that  there was a lot of work that went into  this before the cameras ever started  rolling for you obviously this is you  know you don't see too many people with  with polio and what that comes with  these days so if you can just talk about  the the sort of preparation that you did  which I believe extended even to dental  related items which you can I'll leave  that to you to explain yeah I mean as  soon as it was all go and and Jonathan  decided and and he decided that this was  the right thing I just kind of I just  you know it was like going to university  for Robin cabindish Studies and I had  about two and a half months of getting  to know him by osmosis that's kind of my  favorite part of the acting process is  that that preparation period where  there's no obligation to get anywhere  where you just get to swim around and  spend time in the in the library as it  were and so with that came time with  Jonathan time with Andy and time with  people who are living currently on  ventilation ventilators on external  ventilators doctors my brother's a  doctor in London he introduced me to a  couple of patients of his and the  remarkable thing about that one of the  remarkable things about that is that  every single patient I spoke to had some  connection to Robin and Diana whether it  was a direct connection or whether they  were just aware that their lives had  been changed by these two people's  bravery arguably idiocy and kind of you  know the diving headfirst into you know  putting Robin in in in danger in order  to fully live his life so that was  remarkable and then of course as the  technical challenges and that I worked  closely with Andy and and Jonathan about  that I had one recording we had run one  recording of your father later on in his  life where the croak and his voice  became very  so I we decided well let's work  backwards and we so we start with a  clearer voice and then we end up in that  very kind of deteriorated place and then  the